---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import ServiceHeading from "../../components/ServiceHeading.astro";
import Stamp from "../../components/Stamp.astro";
import { allParts } from "../../lib/catalog";
import { journal } from "../../data/journal.mjs";

const entry = journal.find((item) => item.slug === "sun-visor-clip");
const part = allParts().find((item) => item.slug === entry.partSlug);
if (!part) throw new Error(`journal/${entry.slug}: part ${entry.partSlug} is not in the snapshot`);
const requestHref = `/request/?part=${encodeURIComponent(part.title)}&part_slug=${part.slug}`;
const img = (file: string) => `/images/journal/sun-visor-clip/${file}`;
---
<Base title="Build log 001: Rebuilding the sun visor clip — OCP3D"
      description="How a broken sun visor clip on a 1983 Celica Supra became twelve versions, two deliberate changes from the original, and a v12 in ASA that is back in the car."
      sheet="BUILD LOG 001">
  <article class="entry-page">
    <div class="wrap entry-wrap">
      <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/journal/">Build logs</a><span>/</span><span>Build log 001</span></nav>

      <ServiceHeading
        eyebrow={entry.eyebrow}
        title="Rebuilding the sun visor clip."
        lede="One of the clips that holds the sun visor up on my '83 had broken, and replacements are hard to find. I still had an original to measure. Twelve versions later, v12 is in the car. This is how it got there, including two deliberate changes from the original."
        file="BUILD LOG 001"
        facts={[["VERSIONS", "12"], ["MATERIAL", "ASA"], ["IN THE CAR", "SINCE SEPT 8, 2026"], ["PRINT", "ABOUT 5 G · 21 MIN"]]}
      />

      <figure class="lead-figure">
        <img src={img("v8-vs-v12.jpg")} width="1600" height="1055"
             alt="Two printed visor clips mounted on the same headliner: a boxy orange v8 on the left and a smoother tan v12 on the right." />
        <figcaption>
          <span>LEFT · V8 · ORANGE ASA · AUG 20, 2026</span>
          <span>RIGHT · V12 · TAN ASA · SEPT 8, 2026</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <dl class="entry-facts">
        <div><dt>Part</dt><dd>Sun visor retaining clip</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Car</dt><dd>1983 Toyota Celica Supra (A60), US</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Version</dt><dd>v12 · ASA</dd></div>
        <div><dt>In the car</dt><dd>Sept 8, 2026</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Print</dt><dd>About 5 g · 21 min</dd></div>
        <div><dt>OEM number</dt><dd>Under investigation</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Status</dt><dd><Stamp state={part.state} label={part.stateLabel} /></dd></div>
      </dl>

      <div class="entry-body">
        <section class="entry-section">
          <p class="step-n">01</p>
          <div class="section-copy">
            <h2>The problem</h2>
            <p>This little clip holds the sun visor up against the headliner. After forty-some years the
              plastic gets brittle, and one of mine had broken. Replacements are hard to find, but I still
              had an original to measure, so it became the first part I properly reverse-engineered.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="entry-section">
          <p class="step-n">02</p>
          <div class="section-copy">
            <h2>A part-number question</h2>
            <p>The part number I first found in online diagrams, 74348-12010, turned out to describe a
              different, screw-mounted holder. Mine is a crescent-shaped clip that mounts with a push-in
              fir-tree pin and one screw. The closest candidate so far is the 74336 series, but I haven't
              confirmed it.</p>
            <p>If you have an A60 with this clip and a parts diagram, an old invoice, or a bag with the
              number on it, I'd like to compare notes.</p>
            <p class="inline-cta"><a class="btn btn--secondary" href={requestHref}>Share what you know</a></p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="entry-section">
          <p class="step-n">03</p>
          <div class="section-copy">
            <h2>From photos to calipers (v1 to v5)</h2>
            <p><strong>v1 came from online diagrams and photos only, and it modeled the wrong mechanism
              entirely:</strong> a screw plate with a snap-in channel. Photos can show that a feature exists.
              They can't show what it does.</p>
            <p><strong>v2 came from calipers.</strong> Seven rounds of measuring the original, 18 critical
              dimensions:</p>
            <ul class="ticks">
              <li>the crescent-shaped body</li>
              <li>the C-hook that cradles the 8.1 mm visor rod, with a 7.52 mm throat</li>
              <li>a fir-tree push pin with 12 barbs</li>
              <li>a counterbored screw hole</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>v3 to v5 were the first prints, in PLA and PETG,</strong> held next to the original and
              corrected:</p>
            <ul class="ticks">
              <li>the fir-tree ribs are on both sides, not one</li>
              <li>the hook's jaw came in about 3 mm</li>
              <li>the pin-to-hole spacing was corrected to a measured 9.0 mm</li>
              <li>bevels on the corners, with the edges that locate the part kept crisp</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <figure class="shot shot--cad">
            <img src={img("cad-v12.png")} width="800" height="600" loading="lazy"
                 alt="CAD render of the v12 visor clip: crescent body, C-hook, screw hole, and a six-barb fir-tree pin." />
            <figcaption><span>CAD MODEL · V12</span><span>RENDER, NOT A PHOTO</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="entry-section">
          <p class="step-n">04</p>
          <div class="section-copy">
            <h2>First fit in the car, and two deliberate changes (v5 to v8)</h2>
            <p><strong>Aug 16: v5, printed in PLA, mounted and held the visor,</strong> but the visor had to be
              forced up to hook and unhook it. Measuring showed why: the gap the rod passes through on its way
              in was 7.73 mm, and the rod is 8.10 mm. The original shape is tighter than the rod it has to let
              in.</p>
            <p><strong>Deliberate change 1 (v6):</strong> a shorter lip at the entry turns 0.37 mm of
              interference into 0.62 mm of clearance. The part of the hook that actually holds the rod, the
              7.52 mm throat and the 8.10 mm cradle, didn't change. That makes this a corrected part, not an
              exact copy, and the record says so.</p>
            <p><strong>Deliberate change 2 (v8):</strong> the v7 print came out with its fir-tree barbs fused
              into a ribbed cone. The original has 12 barbs packed into 11.2 mm, which leaves gaps of 0.23 mm,
              narrower than the 0.4 mm nozzle can draw. Injection molding doesn't care about that; FDM printing
              does. v8 has 6 barbs at the same outer diameter, so it grips the same mounting hole with fewer,
              properly formed steps.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="entry-section">
          <p class="step-n">05</p>
          <div class="section-copy">
            <h2>ASA, and making it look right (v8 to v12)</h2>
            <p><strong>Why ASA.</strong> A car interior bakes in the summer, and PLA starts to soften around
              60 °C. ASA holds up to heat (its glass transition is around 100 °C) and to UV. It needs an enclosed
              printer with the chamber warmed up first, so it prints in a Prusa Core One with the vent closed.</p>
            <p><strong>Aug 20: v8 in orange ASA worked, but it looked boxy.</strong> v9 to v12 were about
              looks:</p>
            <ul class="ticks">
              <li>softer edges</li>
              <li>a rounded front</li>
              <li>a clean shelf under the visor opening</li>
              <li>a smoother visor opening itself</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The v12 rounding reaches into the spot where the rod goes in, so it needed its own test in the
              car instead of borrowing v8's result.</p>
            <p><strong>Sept 8: v12 printed in tan ASA,</strong> much closer to the headliner: about 5 g of
              plastic and 21 minutes of printer time. The material under it on the bed is print support, and it
              comes off. Every print is checked before it runs, including that no support ends up inside the
              screw hole.</p>
            <p><strong>Installed the same day.</strong> It mounts solidly, holds the visor up, and the visor
              hooks in and out without forcing it, which is the problem v6 set out to fix.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="shots">
            <figure class="shot shot--portrait">
              <img src={img("v12-on-the-bed.jpg")} width="1205" height="1600" loading="lazy"
                   alt="A tan v12 visor clip on the Prusa Core One print bed, with support material underneath." />
              <figcaption><span>V12 · ON THE BED</span><span>SEPT 8, 2026</span></figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="shot shot--portrait">
              <img src={img("v12-installed.jpg")} width="1205" height="1600" loading="lazy"
                   alt="The tan v12 clip mounted on the headliner above the windshield." />
              <figcaption><span>V12 · INSTALLED</span><span>SEPT 8, 2026</span></figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="shot shot--portrait">
              <video src="/video/journal/visor-v12-snap.mp4" poster={img("v12-snap-poster.jpg")}
                     width="720" height="1280" controls muted loop playsinline preload="none"
                     aria-label="Short silent clip: the visor is pushed up and the tan clip catches and holds its rod."></video>
              <figcaption><span>V12 · VISOR UP, CLIP HOLDS</span><span>SILENT CLIP · 7 S</span></figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="entry-section">
          <p class="step-n">06</p>
          <div class="section-copy">
            <h2>What's next</h2>
            <ul class="ticks">
              <li><strong>Time in the car.</strong> Summer heat and sun are the real test, and I'll report
                back.</li>
              <li><strong>Other cars.</strong> If you have an A60 with a broken or missing clip, I'm looking for
                a few owners to test-fit this one.</li>
              <li><strong>The part number.</strong> Still open. A spare original, even a broken one, helps
                too.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>

      <aside class="evidence-note">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">WHAT THIS RECORD COVERS</p>
          <p>One car (a 1983 US Celica Supra), one clip, one material (ASA), installed Sept 8, 2026. It shows
            the clip fits and holds; it is not a durability claim. The part record carries the measurements and
            the current status.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="note-actions">
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href={`/registry/${part.slug}/`}>See the part record</a>
          <a class="btn btn--secondary" href={requestHref}>Share what you know</a>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .entry-page { background: linear-gradient(180deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--ground-deep) 28%, transparent), transparent 32%); }
  .entry-wrap { display: grid; gap: 18px; }
  .crumbs { display: flex; gap: 8px; flex-wrap: wrap; font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); color: var(--steel); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .1em; }
  .crumbs a { color: var(--ion); }

  .lead-figure { border: var(--bd-inner) solid var(--rule-2); background: var(--ground-deep); }
  .lead-figure img { width: 100%; height: auto; }
  figcaption { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 8px 14px; flex-wrap: wrap; padding: 10px 14px; border-top: var(--bd-inner) solid var(--rule); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-micro); letter-spacing: var(--t-micro-tr); color: var(--steel); }

  .entry-facts { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(150px, 1fr)); margin: 0; border-top: var(--bd-inner) solid var(--rule); border-left: var(--bd-inner) solid var(--rule); background: var(--ground); }
  .entry-facts > div { padding: 14px 16px; border-right: var(--bd-inner) solid var(--rule); border-bottom: var(--bd-inner) solid var(--rule); }
  .entry-facts dt { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-micro); letter-spacing: var(--t-micro-tr); text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--steel); }
  .entry-facts dd { margin: 5px 0 0; color: var(--paper); font-size: var(--t-dense); line-height: var(--t-dense-lh); }

  .entry-body { border: var(--bd-inner) solid var(--rule-2); background: var(--panel); }
  .entry-section { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 58px minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 0 16px; padding: 26px 30px; border-top: var(--bd-inner) solid var(--rule); }
  .entry-section:first-child { border-top: 0; }
  .entry-section > :not(.step-n) { grid-column: 2; }
  .step-n { width: 34px; height: 34px; display: grid; place-items: center; border: 1px solid var(--signal); color: var(--signal); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); letter-spacing: .08em; }
  .section-copy { max-width: 70ch; }
  .section-copy h2 { font-size: 24px; margin-bottom: 12px; }
  .section-copy p { margin-top: 12px; color: var(--body); }
  .section-copy h2 + p { margin-top: 0; }
  .section-copy strong { color: var(--paper); }
  .ticks { display: grid; gap: 7px; margin-top: 10px; color: var(--body); }
  .ticks li { position: relative; padding-left: 18px; }
  .ticks li::before { content: ""; position: absolute; left: 0; top: .72em; width: 8px; border-top: 2px solid var(--signal); }
  .inline-cta { margin-top: 16px; }

  .shots { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 14px; margin-top: 20px; align-items: start; }
  .shot { margin: 0; border: var(--bd-inner) solid var(--rule); background: var(--ground-deep); min-width: 0; }
  .shot img, .shot video { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; background: var(--journal-charcoal); }
  .shot--cad { margin-top: 20px; max-width: 520px; }
  .shot--portrait img, .shot--portrait video { aspect-ratio: 3 / 4; object-fit: cover; }
  .shot--cad-tile img { aspect-ratio: 3 / 4; object-fit: contain; }
  .shot--cad img { background: #f7f7f7; }

  .evidence-note { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto; align-items: center; gap: 22px; padding: 22px 26px; border: var(--bd-inner) solid var(--rule); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ground-deep) 84%, transparent); }
  .evidence-note p:not(.eyebrow) { margin-top: 7px; color: var(--steel); font-size: var(--t-dense); max-width: 70ch; }
  .note-actions { display: flex; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; }

  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .shots { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .evidence-note { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .entry-section { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: 22px 20px; }
    .entry-section > :not(.step-n) { grid-column: 1; }
    .step-n { margin-bottom: 12px; }
    .shots { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .evidence-note { padding: 20px; }
  }
</style>
